The three closest hits:

1. Alstott et al. (2009): Equal-size, 50-node lesions produced location-dependent changes in simulated functional connectivity. This kills a broad “location matters despite equal lesion size” novelty claim. Equal node count is not necessarily equal synaptic mass or delivered drive.
https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pcbi.10004082. Habib et al. (2026, preprint): A fly connectome motor model shows identity- and count-dependent lesion and replacement outcomes. It works with groups of cells and type-dependent rewiring, not the proposed single-cell temporal phenotype.
https://doi.org/10.64898/2026.10.26.7620433. Pugliese et al. (2026): Silencing individual identified interneurons abolishes motor rhythms in a fly connectome rate model. The core-cell necessity result remains a simulation prediction.
https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-026-11195-yVerdict: None I found kills the entire proposed conjunction of single-cell deletion, the specified matched controls, shared motor-pool temporal structure, an intervention-supported relay, and partial drive-envelope locking. But the broad component claims are substantially preceded; the distinction needs to rest on that conjunction and the paper’s actual evidence, not a claim of global priority.

One correction is especially important: Alstott did compare equal-size lesions, contrary to the earlier journal note. I flagged that in the new digest. DNp33 anatomy is documented, but I did not verify a DNp33-specific experimental function or cross-species homolog.
